## Slide 9

Visual Workflow Configuration

## Slide 10

Process Definitions

A Process Definition is a collection of business logic that will be executed by the Workflow Service
Process Definitions support versioning, with the ability to create drafts and to activate/retire versions.
Processes can be Started and Stopped through API Calls. 
Multiple instances of a Process can be running in parallel. Each Process will have independent data and parameters.

## Slide 11

Visual Designer

The Visual Designer provides an interface that eliminates the need to examine script to understand a Workflow
Process Step Definitions can be dragged onto the Visual Designer from the Step Catalogue
Process Path Definitions can be created by dragging Connection Points from one Process Step to another
Containers and Notes can be added to provide comments or additional visual indicators

## Slide 12

Step Types

Pre-Defined Steps exist for common functionality including:
Waiting for certain Entity updates
Waiting a set amount of time, or for a specific time
Sending Notifications
Creating or Waiting for Tasks
Reading/Writing Tag Values
Starting or Waiting for other Processes
Developer Script and SQL parts exist for extensibility

## Slide 13

Process Step Definitions

A Process Step is a distinct set of logic that is run within a parent Process Definition
Each Process Step has configurable options based upon the type of Step
Process Steps can output information to the next step in the Process
Process Steps can read information from the previous step in the Process

## Slide 14

Step Outputs

Each Process Step will produce certain common outputs as well as specifics outputs for the Step Type
Process Step outputs automatically can be used by the next step in the process
Process Step outputs can also be mapped to Process Parameters for use later in the Process

## Slide 15

Paths and Symbols

A Process Path Definition is used to connect two Process Step Definitions
A Process Step Definition can have multiple inbound and/or outbound paths
For each Process Path Definition, conditional requirements can be set to determine when that Path is used
Based upon the inbound and outbound paths, symbols are added to the Process Step Definition to visually identify the resulting logic

Multiple inbound paths
Multiple outbound paths
Conditional outbound paths

## Slide 16

Process Parameter Definitions

Process Parameters Definitions identify values that need to be maintained throughout an entire Process
Process Steps will be able to read/write Parameter values
Each instance of a Process will have independent values for each Parameter definitions

